Approach 1: Brute Force – Delete Each Character
Idea:
1. Check whether the original string is a palindrome.
2. If not, try deleting each character one by one.
3. Check whether the resulting string is a palindrome.
4. If any resulting string is a palindrome, return true.

Complexity analysis:
- Checking the original string: (O(n)).
- Creating a string after deletion: (O(n)).
- Checking whether it is a palindrome: (O(n)).
- Repeating for all characters: (O(n^2)).


class Solution {
public:
    bool isPalindrome(string s) {
        int l = 0, r = s.size() - 1;

        while (l < r) {
            if (s[l] != s[r]) return false;
            l++;
            r--;
        }

        return true;
    }

    bool validPalindrome(string s) {
        int n = s.size();

        if (isPalindrome(s)) return true;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            string t = s.substr(0, i) + s.substr(i + 1);

            if (isPalindrome(t)) return true;
        }

        return false;
    }
};


Two Pointers + Index Ranges (Optimal)
Recommended approach
Idea:

We can eliminate the extra substring creation.

Instead of creating two new strings, simply pass the starting and ending indices to the palindrome-checking function.

At the first mismatch:
- Check l + 1, r (skip left).
- Check l, r - 1 (skip right).
If either is a palindrome, return true.
Complexity	Value
Time	(O(n))
Auxiliary space	(O(1))
The string is passed by reference, and we only use a constant number of variables.


class Solution {
public:
    bool check(string &s, int l, int r) {
        while (l < r) {
            if (s[l] != s[r]) return false;

            l++;
            r--;
        }

        return true;
    }

    bool validPalindrome(string s) {
        int l = 0, r = s.size() - 1;

        while (l < r) {
            if (s[l] != s[r]) {
                return check(s, l + 1, r) ||check(s, l, r - 1);
            }

            l++;
            r--;
        }

        return true;
    }
};
